FINAL: This report examines the global renewable energy landscape in 2025 and 2026, detailing unprecedented capacity expansions, technological breakthroughs, and regional market trends. It is organized into sections covering global investment and capacity milestones, key technological innovations in solar, wind, and storage, and the policy and market challenges shaping the sector's future.

## Global and Regional Renewable Energy Trends, Market Outlook, and Technological Innovations

### Global Energy Landscape and Market Growth
According to the Renewables Global Status Report (GSR) 2025—marking its 20th edition from REN21—the energy sector remains characterized by ongoing progress alongside systemic challenges and a persistent dependence on fossil fuels. Greenfueljournal.com reports that 2026 marks a historical turning point where wind, solar, hydro, and other clean sources collectively overtake coal as the world's largest electricity generation source. The International Energy Agency (IEA) notes that wind and solar alone now supply nearly 20% of global electricity, compared to 4% a decade ago. Global energy transition investment reached a record $2.3 trillion in 2025, more than double the investment in fossil fuel supply, with BloombergNEF recording this investment baseline. Projections indicate that renewables will account for 43% of global electricity generation by 2030, with cumulative capacity tracking toward 9,530 GW, while solar PV is expected to represent 80% of new capacity between 2025 and 2030, and wind 15%. According to startus-insights.com, global solar PV module prices have fallen 90% since 2009, while wind turbine prices have dropped 49-78% since 2010, and 2024 renewable capacity additions reached approximately 582-585 GW, accounting to 92.5% of all new power capacity globally. Regional trends highlight that China accounts for 60% of global growth, India's capacity is set to rise 2.5 times by 2030, and utility-scale solar generation in the United States is projected to rise 17% in 2026.

### United States Policy Shifts and Market Dynamics
The 2026 Renewable Energy Industry Outlook from Deloitte details a significant shift in the United States renewable energy landscape driven by policy changes in 2025, specifically the "One Big Beautiful Bill Act" (OBBBA). This legislation rolled back clean energy tax credits and imposed stricter regulations, contributing to an 18% decline in wind and solar investments during the first half of 2025 compared to the same period in 2024, totaling nearly US$35 billion. Despite this, renewables accounted for 93% of new capacity additions in the U.S. through September 2025, reaching 30.2 gigawatts, with solar and storage representing 83% of that growth. The OBBBA shortened qualification windows for wind and solar credits and introduced new Internal Revenue Service requirements regarding continuous construction. Furthermore, new foreign entity of concern (FEOC) sourcing rules restricting entities linked to China, Russia, Iran, and North Korea have increased supply chain pressures. Deloitte projects annual solar, wind, and storage additions between 2026 and 2030 could decline to a range of 30 GW to 66 GW, compared to pre-OBBBA projections of 54 GW to 85 GW, while phaseout policies are expected to increase solar costs by 36% to 55% and onshore wind costs by 32% to 63% over the coming year. Additional headwinds include a Department of the Interior pause in offshore wind leasing, funding reductions for agency siting and financing, and a proposed Environmental Protection Agency repeal of fossil plant emissions standards. Antidumping and countervailing duties on solar imports from four Southeast Asian countries have reached as high as 3,404%. State-level momentum is uneven: while 28 states with renewable portfolio standards (RPS) drove 37% of renewable additions in 2024, Ohio is set to sunset its RPS after 2026, and North Carolina has rolled back its 2030 carbon reduction target.

### Investment, Corporate Demand, and Deal Activity
Despite headwinds, demand from hyperscalers drives growth in firm, low-carbon power, with the U.S. hosting 90% of global carbon-free energy contracts for hyperscalers, where 78% of that energy is supplied by renewables. By October 2025, U.S. operating storage capacity reached 37.4 GW (a 32% increase year-to-date), with a 187 GW pipeline projected by 2030; in the Southwest Power Pool (SPP), 10 GW of storage is estimated to avoid US$2.2 billion in system costs over the next decade. In the first nine months of 2025, the U.S. saw 58 announced renewable deals worth US$6 billion, representing a 41% decrease in value and a 45% drop in volume compared to the previous year. Platform acquisitions increased 4.6x in value, whereas asset-level deals declined by 89% in volume during the first eight months of 2025. Approximately 76% of U.S. power and renewable executives planned to increase AI spending in 2025 to support operational efficiency, standardized designs, and compliance management. Corporate power purchase agreements (PPAs) now drive 30% of global renewable capacity expansion.

### Technological Innovations in Solar, Wind, and Energy Storage
Global technology advancements span multiple domains:
- **Solar Energy:** Oxford PV perovskite-silicon tandem modules reach 26.9% efficiency (compared to 21-23% for typical silicon modules), with lab-scale perovskite cells exceeding 33% efficiency and expected to exceed 30% efficiency by 2026 alongside roll-to-roll manufacturing pilots. Bifacial cells captured 90% of the global market in 2024, providing 5-25% additional energy compared to monofacial panels. Floating photovoltaic (FPV) systems, such as the 60 MWp Sembcorp Tengeh farm, reduce reservoir evaporation by up to 60%.
- **Wind Energy:** Offshore wind turbines reach capacities of 15 MW to 20 MW. Floating offshore wind features 245 MW operational across 15 projects in 7 countries as of late 2024, employing spar, semi-submersible, and tension-leg designs for deep waters. Blade recycling innovations like Siemens Gamesa’s RecyclableBlade enable glass and carbon fiber reclamation (noting 85-90% of a turbine's mass is already recyclable), while bladeless concepts like Vortex Bladeless and Aeromine utilize oscillation or static aerofoils. Airborne wind systems using kites or drones target high-altitude winds.
- **Hydropower and Geothermal:** Hydropower remains the largest grid-scale storage source with 200 GW of pumped-storage hydropower (PSH) installed. Innovations include RheEnergise’s R-19 high-density hydro fluid (2.5 times denser than water for low-hill PSH) and Natel Energy’s FishSafe turbine designs addressing the 22.3% mean mortality rate of conventional turbines. Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS) within an 8 km depth could generate 300,000 exajoules of electricity, with binary organic Rankine cycle plants accounting to 25.1% of global geothermal capacity.
- **Energy Storage & Bioenergy:** The Levelized Cost of Electricity (LCOE) for a four-hour battery storage project reached a record low of $78/MWh in early 2026. Solid-state batteries offer energy densities exceeding 400 Wh/kg using solid electrolytes to suppress dendrite formation. Long-duration energy storage (LDES) explores iron-air, compressed air, and gravity-based systems. Bioenergy enhancements include AI-driven logistics with 90% accuracy in feedstock quality assessment and bioenergy with carbon capture and storage (BECCS), though current geological storage remains under 1 MtCO2/yr. Green hydrogen scales via PEM units (for dynamic solar/wind inputs) and alkaline electrolyzers (for steady baseload applications).

### Sector Bottlenecks and Grid Modernization
The sector faces critical challenges including grid congestion (over 2,600 GW in U.S. interconnection queues and European permitting timelines stretching up to 15 years), supply chain concentration (China processes over 80% of global rare earth elements and dominates lithium, cobalt, and manganese refining), and a "green skills gap" involving a shortage of solar installers, wind technicians, and electrical engineers. Grid modernization relies on AI-driven predictive maintenance, wide-area monitoring using phasor measurement units, dynamic line rating based on real-time weather data, and virtual power plants aggregating distributed energy resources (DERs) like rooftop solar, home batteries, and EV chargers. Nuclear fusion research continues with the Q>1 physics threshold benchmark for commercial viability, aimed at the 2030s.

