## Global Energy Trends and Renewable Capacity Additions in 2025

According to the International Energy Agency's *Global Energy Review 2026*, all major energy fuels and technologies grew in 2025 at varying rates. Overall global energy demand growth slowed to 1.3%, falling just below the previous decade's average. This deceleration was driven by slower economic growth, reduced energy-intensive industry activity in certain regions, lower cooling demand, and faster efficiency improvements. Solar photovoltaic (PV) met more than 25% of higher demand as the largest single source of growth, followed by natural gas at 17%, marking the first time on record that a modern renewable source provided the largest share of global energy demand growth. Demand for oil, natural gas, and coal all grew in 2025 but at slower rates than in 2024, while low-emissions sources combined—including solar, wind, nuclear, hydropower, and other renewables—contributed nearly 60% of global demand growth.

United States energy demand growth rose to its second-highest level since 2000, excluding post-recession rebound years, supported by strong electricity demand from data centres, robust industrial growth, and colder temperatures. In contrast, China accounted for the largest overall share of global energy demand growth, but its growth rate slowed sharply to 1.7% due to the rapid expansion of renewables and efficiency gains. Global electricity demand grew at well over twice the rate of energy demand at nearly 3%, remaining above the previous decade's 2.8% average but slowing from 2024 due to one-off factors like lower cooling demand in India and Southeast Asia. Electric vehicles and data centres grew rapidly, with data centres making up half of all electricity use growth in the United States. 

Oil demand growth slowed further to an increase of 0.65 million barrels per day (mb/d), or 0.7%, down from 2024's 0.75 mb/d growth and well below the 2010–2019 annual average of 1.4 mb/d. This reflected weaker petrochemical feedstock growth in China and the moderating effect of electric car sales climbing over 20% to more than 20 million units, representing around one-quarter of new car sales in 2025. Natural gas demand growth slowed markedly in 2025, rising by about 1% down from 2.8% in 2024 amid relatively high prices in the first half of the year. Incremental gas demand was concentrated in the United States, the European Union (supported by colder winter weather), and the Middle East (where power sector gas use grew quickly), while Asia Pacific demand grew at its weakest pace since the 2022 energy crisis. Coal demand grew modestly by around 0.4% above 2024 levels: in the United States, gas-to-coal switching and strong electricity demand growth supported a 10% rise reversing recent declines; coal demand was flat in China where renewables pushed down power sector coal use and lower industrial use in steel and cement production was offset by chemical manufacturing; and coal demand decreased in India mostly due to an early, strong, and long monsoon.

Renewable and nuclear generation increases exceeded total electricity supply growth in 2025. Solar PV increased by 600 terawatt-hours (TWh)—representing the largest-ever electricity generation increase by any source in a single year outside of post-crisis recoveries—meeting around 70% of electricity generation growth. Renewables combined now virtually match total global generation from coal. In the European Union, the share of solar PV and wind reached 30% in 2025, surpassing fossil fuels for the first time. Global annual renewable capacity additions reached a record 800 gigawatts (GW) according to the IEA (while IRENA reports 692 GW), with solar contributing 75%. Battery storage capacity additions rose around 40% to reach almost 110 GW, surpassing the highest-ever annual natural gas capacity additions, and construction started on over 12 GW of nuclear power capacity. 

Global energy-related carbon dioxide emissions grew by around 0.4%. Emissions fell in China due to renewable booms and structural declines in energy-intensive industries, and remained flat in India for the first time since the 1970s due to strong monsoons and renewables growth. Meanwhile, advanced economy emissions grew faster (+0.5%) than emerging market and developing economy emissions (+0.3%) for the first time since the 1990s due to a cold winter and higher natural gas prices. The rollout of clean energy technologies since 2019 avoided more than 35 exajoules of annual fossil fuel demand in 2025 (about 7% of global fossil fuel use) and prevented 3 billion tonnes of CO2 annually (about 8% of global emissions). Avoided coal demand of around 800 million tonnes of coal equivalent exceeded India's entire 2025 coal use, and estimated avoided gas demand of over 260 billion cubic metres equated to almost half the global liquefied natural gas market.

